WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
High impact is the strongest commonly discussed FedRAMP baseline category.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.250
It is associated with systems where the loss of confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.715
or availability could have severe or catastrophic adverse effects.

00:00:11.715 --> 00:00:14.810
That might involve highly sensitive data, major mission dependency,

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.350
serious operational disruption,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.744
or consequences that extend beyond routine business inconvenience.

00:00:19.744 --> 00:00:23.680
High should be chosen because the risk calls for it, not because it sounds impressive.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.300
High baseline planning requires more than adding extra rows to a spreadsheet.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.820
It tends to increase expectations for resilience, privileged access control, separation,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.810
auditability, incident response maturity, contingency planning, testing,

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.160
and control effectiveness.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.020
The architecture may need stronger segmentation, more mature recovery practices,

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.545
better automation, tighter operational governance,

00:00:43.545 --> 00:00:47.220
and clearer evidence trails for how critical security functions are performed.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.840
The operational burden is real.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.335
Teams supporting High-impact services need mature change management, reliable monitoring,

00:00:53.335 --> 00:00:57.290
disciplined access reviews, rapid vulnerability handling, tested incident processes,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.825
and strong alignment between engineering, security, compliance, and operations.

00:01:01.825 --> 00:01:05.250
Evidence collection should be built into normal workflows rather than assembled by memory

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.280
after the fact.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.580
Manual, undocumented,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.800
or hero-driven operations do not scale well into higher-impact expectations.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.220
High is not automatically better than Moderate or Low.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.700
The right baseline is the one that matches the mission and data risk.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.365
Selecting too low a baseline can leave agencies underprotected.

00:01:20.365 --> 00:01:22.950
Selecting too high a baseline can create cost, delay,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.600
and commitments that do not match the use case.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.230
The professional approach is to make an impact-based decision and then build the system,

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.210
evidence, and operations to support that decision honestly.
